  "difficulty_reason": "The counted proof lies above ordinary qualification differential geometry because SL(4,R)/SL(3,R) is not a symmetric space and the bracket of two transversal components has a non-zero scalar component, so the general flat-extension theorem does not apply automatically. The authors lift a volume-preserving frame to an SL(4,R) frame using the chosen transversal, recover theta=F^*(mu^top) from the volume compatibility of the immersion, and deduce flatness of the transversal connection from the volume-form lemma. They then read the transversal component of the pulled-back Maurer-Cartan equation to see that the scalar part of the bracket vanishes, which supplies the hypothesis of the counted flat-extension theorem and yields vanishing of the Chern-Simons invariant.",
